WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.480
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.355
ROGER MARTINEZ: A secure
Google Cloud project

00:00:02.355 --> 00:00:04.530
is a happy Google Cloud
project, and a big part

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:08.100
of maintaining that security is
securing your service accounts.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.083
Remember, access
to resources can

00:00:10.083 --> 00:00:12.000
be granted to service
accounts in the same way

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.370
that they can be
granted to users.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:16.920
And not only are they designed
to be used by applications

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.860
and virtual machines, they can
be accessed by other users,

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.060
too.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:22.560
That means that
a service account

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.170
that's set up with access
to too many resources

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:27.150
and that's accessible
by too many users

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:30.370
introduces a serious
vulnerability.

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:32.685
So in this episode,
we'll talk about what's

00:00:32.685 --> 00:00:34.710
what with securing your
service account's roles

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:36.660
and permissions.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.300
When thinking about securely
managing service accounts,

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:42.270
you could start by
asking two questions--

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:43.980
what resources can
your service accounts

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.980
access, and what permissions
do your service accounts need?

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:51.230
For example, let's take
this service account

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:53.330
with the editor role assigned.

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:56.630
What resources can this
service account access?

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:58.520
Well, with the editor
role, the answer

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:01.160
is pretty much all of them.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:04.730
OK, so what permissions does
this service account need?

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:06.500
The answer to that
will depend on what

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.420
you need the service
account to do,

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:11.540
and it's likely to be a lot less
than the permissions associated

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:13.220
with the editor role.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.830
This means that you'll have to
adjust the project's IAM policy

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:19.550
to delegate the appropriate
IAM roles and permissions.

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:22.100
When you do that, it's best
to adhere to the principle

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.080
of least privilege.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:25.970
That means assigning
to your service account

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:29.090
the minimum access required for
it to carry out its intended

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:30.650
function.

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:32.900
Any account with permissions
beyond what it needs

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:35.940
introduces an unwanted
security risk.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.570
So steer clear of assigning
broad roles to your service

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:40.850
accounts, like owner
or editor, especially

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:42.930
in a production environment.

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:45.128
This is true even for
default service accounts that

00:01:45.128 --> 00:01:47.420
are created for you with the
editor role assigned right

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:48.380
off the bat.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:50.150
Yeah, it's useful
for development.

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:53.390
But make sure to narrow things
down when turning things up

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:55.890
for production.

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:57.600
Applying the principle
of least privilege

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.090
also means limiting
which users have access

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:01.860
to your service accounts.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.650
Access to your service accounts
can mean different things

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:07.380
since there are a number
of predefined IAM roles

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.930
that will grant users
permissions over your service

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:11.787
accounts in different ways.

00:02:11.787 --> 00:02:13.370
Let's talk about
some of these service

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:16.920
account-related roles that might
impact your project security.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.520
First up, we have the Create
Service Account and Delete

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.170
Service Account roles.

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:24.930
They allow users to do
exactly what their names say--

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:28.140
create new service accounts
and delete existing service

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:30.160
accounts, respectively.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:32.400
It's worth noting that
the Create role on its own

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.010
doesn't let you do anything
beyond creating a new service

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:35.530
account.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:37.530
You'll need to be granted
additional permissions

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:41.820
to make changes to its roles
or to manage its IAM policy.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:44.130
Likewise, the
Delete role does not

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:47.610
include the permissions to
undelete, disable, or enable,

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:49.740
only delete service accounts.

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:53.070
Up next is that Service
Account User role.

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:54.780
With this role,
users are allowed

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:57.940
to act as a service account
to run certain operations.

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:00.090
For example, a user
that has the permission

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:03.330
to create Compute Instances,
Cloud Functions, or AppEngine

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:05.360
Apps will need the
service account user

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:08.040
role assigned so that they can
attach a service account to any

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:09.600
of those resources.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.420
Next there's Service
Account Admin.

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:15.570
A user with this role can do all
administrative tasks on service

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:18.690
accounts-- enable them,
disable them, delete them,

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:20.700
and update their details.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:23.400
They can even make changes
to the service account IAM

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.940
policies so a user of this role
can delegate access to service

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.280
accounts to other users.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.920
For that reason, the
Service Account Admin role

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:35.010
is very powerful and should
be delegated very carefully,

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:36.850
if at all.

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:38.970
Another administrative
role for service accounts

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:42.130
is a Service Account
Key Admin role.

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:44.850
Users of this role will be
able to create and delete

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:46.950
service account private keys.

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:48.930
These keys can be
used by other users

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:51.630
to run commands as a
corresponding service account.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:55.030
As you can probably guess,
this can become problematic,

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:57.420
especially if you have a
boatload of keys floating

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:00.420
around without an effective way
of tracking them or managing

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.188
their distribution.

00:04:02.188 --> 00:04:03.730
This is another role
that you'll want

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:05.590
to delegate very carefully.

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:09.430
And finally, there is a
Service Account Token Creator.

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:11.450
With this role, a
user is able to use

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:14.440
the impersonate-service-account
flag to run gcloud commands

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:16.505
as a specified service account.

00:04:16.505 --> 00:04:18.130
Now, this kind of
impersonation doesn't

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:20.079
require the use of
any private keys

00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:23.050
and is considered a
safer alternative.

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:24.730
The Service Account
Token Creator role

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:27.880
also lets users create
OAuth2 access tokens,

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:32.810
sign blobs, or generate
self-signed JSON Web Tokens.

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:36.100
The Service Account User, Admin,
Key Admin, and Token Creator

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:38.320
roles I mention could be
granted to any account

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:41.230
by adding the binding to
a project's IAM policy.

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:42.670
But watch out.

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:44.387
By granting them on
a project's policy,

00:04:44.387 --> 00:04:46.720
it will grant those permissions
on every service account

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:48.708
that exists on that project.

00:04:48.708 --> 00:04:50.500
Now, this likely goes
against the principle

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:54.370
of least privilege and is
generally not a great idea.

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:57.490
Instead, you can avoid
overdelegating access

00:04:57.490 --> 00:05:00.820
to service accounts by editing
individual service account IAM

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:02.530
policies.

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:04.740
Service accounts
aren't just accounts.

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:06.115
They're also
considered resources

00:05:06.115 --> 00:05:09.190
with an attached IAM policy.

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:12.368
By binding users to these roles
on individual service accounts,

00:05:12.368 --> 00:05:14.410
you will decrease the
likelihood that a user will

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:17.590
make an accidental change
and substantially decrease

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:21.530
the blast radius if a user
account is ever compromised.

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:24.290
Do your best to grant users
access only to the service

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:26.450
accounts that they
actually need.

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:29.030
It's also worth noting
that yes, service accounts

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:31.640
can be granted these roles
on other service accounts.

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:34.280
So take into account
instances where

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:37.340
a user can have indirect
access to a service account

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:38.810
through another service account.

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:41.180
You can see a service
account's IAM policy

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:45.320
with gcloud iam service-accounts
get-iam-policy followed

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:46.550
by a service account.

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:48.470
By understanding
the roles associated

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:50.180
with service
accounts and by doing

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:52.430
a thorough review
of your IAM policies

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:54.380
to apply the principle
of least privilege,

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:56.540
you'll be taking major
steps towards maintaining

00:05:56.540 --> 00:05:58.370
a safe and secure project.

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:00.110
But that's not all.

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:02.180
Even after you've done
that, there's still more

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:05.030
you need to know about
securing your service accounts.

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:06.770
Next time, we'll spend
some time talking

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:08.930
about securely impersonating
service accounts

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:11.750
and effectively managing
their private keys.

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:12.440
See you there.

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:15.790
[MUSIC PLAYING]

